WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:03.900
Hey Wisecrack!

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.060
Flesh Jared again.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:09.580
Before we get into it, just a note: We didn’t
do a quicktake last week because, well, while

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.380
The Whirly Dirly Conspiracy was one of our
favorite episodes of the season, we didn’t

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.000
really have much to add to the discussion.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.740
But we’re back to take on Season 3 episode
6, where we’ll be looking at the toxic side

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.699
of our favorite adventuring duo.

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:24.160
I really love this episode for two reasons.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.119
First, they manage to address the kind of
character development we crave in a

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:33.320
way that is both satisfying and the exact
opposite of what regular TV does.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.190
And second, they tap into some really interesting
ideas about mental health.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.640
So fire your therapist and join me on this
Quick Take of Rest and Ricklaxation.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.500
A quick recap: Rick and Morty need a break
from almost dying all the time

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.520
and come to the realization that they’re burned out.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:59.760
So they vacation at an alien spa, and take
a mental detoxifier for a spin.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.970
While the duo feel cleansed and rejuvenated,
they leave behind the parts they hate most

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.259
about themselves.

00:01:05.259 --> 00:01:09.179
Morty continues to live his life without the
sense of crippling self-doubt, becomes the

00:01:09.179 --> 00:01:12.979
most popular kid in school, and even lands
a date with Jessica.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.840
Meanwhile, Rick seems like a normal human
being.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.140
But this new reality is interrupted as the
toxic versions of themselves manage to escape

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.680
and turn the whole world, uh, weird.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:35.160
One of the cleverest aspects of this episode
is what I’m going to call “anti-character development.”

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.020
As we’ve mentioned before, Dan Harmon is
a strict adherent to his version of Joseph

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.889
Campbell’s “The Hero’s Journey.”

00:01:41.889 --> 00:01:46.479
The relevant part here is that many story’s
use protagonists confronting their “shadow,”

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:51.340
a kind of dark reflection of themselves that
they must overcome - Like Dr. Jekyll and Mr.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:52.340
Hyde.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.329
In terms of character development, this overcoming
usually means our hero has some new insight

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:05.100
on life - like when a compassionate Logan sacrifices himself battling the murderous X-24 -overcoming his selfishness,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.180
or when Luke Skywalker spares Darth Vader, overcoming his temptation towards the Dark Side.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.220
This episode uses this structure, toxic Morty
and toxic Rick are quite literal “shadow”

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.580
versions of themselves.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:21.900
But instead of overcoming their toxic personality
traits, this episode is a journey to reunite

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.000
with their worst selves.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:31.080
Healthy-Morty is a smooth operator with lots of friends
who ultimately needs to rediscover his self

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:36.659
loathing, and Healthy-Rick is a thoughtful super-genius
who needs to rediscover his narcissism and

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.640
irrational care for Morty.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.820
If anything, the “regular” kind of 
character development

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.680
Is pretty creepy

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:53.980
But I also think this “anti character development”
serves as a pretty smart commentary on mental

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.530
health in our society.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.780
Rest and Ricklaxation asks: What does it mean
to be mentally healthy?

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:07.360
In many ways, this episode serves as an extended
parody of certain “healthy” attitudes

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.230
promoted by self-help gurus, popular media
and the occasional “life purpose cheerleader"

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.980
- I swear, they exist in LA.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.319
In particular, this idea that we must strive
to eliminate any thoughts that are unpleasant,

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:22.400
or painful.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.240
#positivevibes #selfcare #blessed #sorrynotsorry.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.820
Now, there are a ton of Insta-llectuals who
will tell you that being mentally healthy

00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:35.599
means uncritically loving yourself, saying
yes to everything, counting your blessings,

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:39.840
and believing in yourself no matter how stupid
your passive income scheme is.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.530
However, Rick and Morty shows us that these
ideals, fully embodied, will make you seem

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.540
like a coked-out weirdo.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.520
I think one of the ideas the show is getting
at is something sociologist William Davies

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.540
has written about in his book “The Happiness
Industry.”

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.680
For our long-time fans, we actually talked
about this book in our Inside Out video.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.939
Davies writes about a concept he calls “compulsory
happiness,” that in our modern world there

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:12.980
exists a kind of alliance between corporations,
managers and advertisers to promote a definition

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:17.650
of mental health that ultimately serves the
capitalist society that we live in; a kind

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:23.340
of happiness that involves cheerfully accepting
the status quo, something touched on by Rick before.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.720
This kind of happiness also requires constant
self improvement for the sake of being

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:35.740
a better consumer.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.780
We see Morty spouting platitudes that seem
lifted from a Tony Robbins book,

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.200
demanding that everyone around him partake in his new
lifestyle.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.300
And, when confronted with real life problems,
like a sentient part of himself being trapped

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.960
in hell, his answer is “Trust me things
are good.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.030
Taking that away from me… that wouldn’t
be healthy.”

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:15.800
The problem with this is that many of the
things the self-help industry wants you to

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.680
erase, like self-doubt and sadness, are pretty
important.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.810
In the case of Morty, it can hold you back
from becoming a full-on sociopath.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.960
And a sociopath he becomes.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.220
In a scene lifted from the Wolf of Wall Street,
it looks like “healthy” Morty is at the

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.720
top of his game as an unscrupulous stockbroker,

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.320
with a new hot redhead to replace Jessica.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:46.620
Rick even compares him to another sociopathic
Wall Street fat cat with too much self-esteem.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.960
So what about Rick?

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:54.720
Rick realizes some of these less desirable
parts of ourselves, despite the pain they

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.720
cause, are essential to actually being healthy

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.780
Jessica even tells Morty, "Good to have you back."

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.960
suggesting that
Morty’s authentic, self-hating self, is

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:07.919
truly his better self.

00:06:07.919 --> 00:06:12.479
But my only complaint with this episode is, well, it
doesn’t really make sense.

00:06:12.479 --> 00:06:17.139
We’re meant to believe that the undesirable
parts of a person are purged from their being

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.400
based on their own subjective definition of
toxicity.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:33.500
Yet, “Healthy Rick” says this to morty:
"I’m really proud to be your grandpa", then

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:38.070
later, claims that his attachment to Morty
is irrational, therefore, toxic, and allows

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.120
him to nonchalantly do this.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.460
So is his love for Morty toxic or healthy?

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.040
Well for more thoughts on that, be sure to
check out The Squanch, our very own Rick and

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.040
Morty podcast.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.259
We’ve been breaking down every episode of
Rick and Morty, and people seem to like it.

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:57.150
For those of you missing a quicktake on the
Whirly Dirly conspiracy, we’ve even got

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.150
an episode on that.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:02.210
You can find a link in the description, or
almost anywhere you get your podcasts, iTunes,

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:05.250
Stitchr, Google Play, SoundCloud - just to name
a few.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.419
And don’t forget to leave a review on iTunes,
we might even read it on air.

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:09.550
Thanks for watching guys.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:10.050
Peace!

